76a11e43-9b25-4bad-bfcf-f2f4fcd6a508	67bc1fd2-36e5-4f62-9905-ec2197cde90d	44	SOLID is an acronym representing five design principles—Single Responsibility, Open/Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation, and Dependency Inversion—that guide us toward building maintainable object-oriented systems. For Single Responsibility Principle, the key idea is that a class should have only one reason to change. Instead of having a single 'ReportGenerator' class handle data fetching, formatting, and saving, we should separate these into distinct classes like `DataFetcher`, `Formatter`, and `Repository`. This separation ensures that if our save mechanism changes, we only modify the repository without touching the core logic.	The answer must demonstrate comprehensive knowledge by listing all principles and providing a concrete 'before/after' scenario for the example to show true understanding of separation of concerns.	surgical-feedback-v1.4	f	[{"id": "TD1", "name": "Foundational Knowledge", "score": 50, "weight": 0.4444444444444445}, {"id": "TD2", "name": "Practical Application", "score": 40, "weight": 0.5555555555555556}]	2026-07-03 02:32:59.607+07
