98f71562-f4f0-40b1-8e06-c1c7f1cfe180	7a039e05-2ec7-4b09-8ba3-f5e951cacf17	64	SOLID stands for five design principles—Single Responsibility, Open/Closed, Liskov Substitution, Interface Segregation, and Dependency Inversion—which guide us toward maintainable code. The Single Responsibility Principle dictates that a class should only have one reason to change. For example, instead of having a single `User` class handle both user data validation *and* sending email notifications, we should separate those concerns into distinct classes like `UserValidator` and `EmailService`. This ensures that if the notification method changes, we don't risk breaking the core user model.	While you defined the principles accurately, your answer lacked a concrete, actionable real-world example to demonstrate practical application, which is critical for this type of technical question.	surgical-feedback-v1.4	f	[{"id": "TD1", "name": "Foundational Knowledge", "score": 90, "weight": 0.3076923076923077}, {"id": "TD2", "name": "Practical Application", "score": 35, "weight": 0.3846153846153846}, {"id": "TD4", "name": "Code Quality & Best Practices", "score": 75, "weight": 0.3076923076923077}]	2026-07-03 11:29:49.714+07
